  "2c2e5932-6310-4ebf-968d-27342af2d988": {
    "pdf_name": "CAPSTONE_REPORT.pdf",
    "created_at": "2026-07-18T11:17:17.575374",
    "chat_history": [
      {
        "question": "what is this pdf about?",
        "answer": "I don't know. The context appears to be a technical or academic document, but it doesn't explicitly state the main topic or subject of the PDF.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "page": 49,
            "text": "A key improvement in this system is the handling of red colours.\nExisting literature and industry recommendations suggest that red and dark\nred colours are universally compatible with all undertones [22, 25, 27, 26, 28].\nBut the exact hue direction in the red family is dependent on the undertone:\n36"
          },
          {
            "page": 57,
            "text": "hashing methods implemented by ImageHash [38], ensuring a diverse set\nof images are used to avoid any redundancy.\nThis dataset is only used to test the enhancement pipeline and no\nmachine learning components of the system were trained using this data.\n44"
          },
          {
            "page": 36,
            "text": "Figure 4.1: Overall system architecture of the proposed framework.\nTo better understand the workflow, Figure 4.2 illustrates a condensed\nview of the execution of the pipeline with an example. Where the archi-\ntecture diagram is an organisational view of the system, this figure is a\nfunctional view of how visual data and outputs flow and are transformed\nthroughout the system.\n23"
          },
          {
            "page": 86,
            "text": "the potential to manipulate visual attention, rather than merely altering ap-\npearance. The statistical significance, Wilcoxon signed-rank test: p=0.004;\nbinomial test: p \u00a1 0.001, also supports the idea that this effect is real and not\nattributable to chance.\nOn the other hand, the variability across images is consistent with\nnatural viewing environments, where attention is not only driven by the\nvisual properties of the face, but also by the surrounding context, including\nsaliency of the background, pose and competition between multiple faces.\n73"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "pdf_names": [
      "CAPSTONE_REPORT.pdf"
    ]
  },
  "157bf128-b307-4fa1-a8b4-f542053953e6": {
    "pdf_name": "WeNext_Meta_WhatsApp_FAQ.pdf",
    "created_at": "2026-07-18T15:49:41.951552",
    "chat_history": [
      {
        "question": "who is narendra modi?",
        "answer": "I don't know.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "page": 1,
            "text": "2.1  What is Business Verification? \nBusiness Verification is Meta\u2019s process of confirming that your business is a legitimate, \nregistered entity. It is mandatory to unlock higher messaging tiers, send certain template \ncategories, and use authentication templates (OTPs). \n2.2  What documents does Meta accept for verification? \n\u2022GST registration certificate \n\u2022Certificate of Incorporation \n\u2022Business license or trade license \n\u2022Utility bill in the business name (electricity, phone, water) \n\u2022Bank statement showing business name \n2.3  How long does verification take? \nTypically 2\u20135 business days. It can take up to 14 days if documents are unclear, incomplete, or \nif Meta requires additional review. Indian businesses with GST certificates usually get approved \nfaster."
          },
          {
            "page": 6,
            "text": "6.2  What is the minimum activity required? \n\u2022At least 1 approved template in your WABA \n\u2022Minimum template sends to real users to establish a quality signal (Meta does not \npublish an exact number but WeNext recommends at least 500 delivered messages) \n\u2022Quality rating must be Medium or High \n\u2022Business Verification must be complete \n\u2022Payment method must be active \n6.3  What is a free entry point (FEP) and how does it help? \nA Free Entry Point is when a user initiates a conversation with your business via a Click-to-\nWhatsApp (CTWA) ad on Facebook or Instagram, or via a WhatsApp button on your Facebook \nPage. When this happens: \n\u2022A 72-hour messaging window opens \n\u2022All template messages sent within this window are free (no Meta charges)"
          },
          {
            "page": 0,
            "text": "WeNext.ai\nMeta & WhatsApp Business Platform\nComplete FAQ for Clients  \u2014  Updated March 2026\n1. Meta Business Manager Setup \n1.1  What is Meta Business Manager and why do I need it? \nMeta Business Manager (now called Meta Business Portfolio) is your central hub for all \nMeta assets \u2014 your Facebook Page, WhatsApp Business Account (WABA), ad accounts, \nand payment methods. You cannot connect to the WhatsApp Business API without an \nactive, verified Business Manager. \n1.2  How do I create a Meta Business Manager? \n1.Go to business.facebook.com and click \u201cCreate Account\u201d \n2.Enter your business name, your name, and business email \n3.Add your Facebook Page to the Business Manager \n4.Complete Business Verification (see Section 2)"
          },
          {
            "page": 6,
            "text": "7.2  Can I use my existing WhatsApp Business App number? \nYes, but you must migrate it. The number must be disconnected from the WhatsApp Business \nApp before it can be registered on the API. This process deletes your WhatsApp Business App \nhistory on that number. WeNext can guide you through the migration process. \n7.3  What is a display name and how is it approved? \nWeNext guidance: We recommend clients run a simple utility or authentication template campaign to \n500\u20131,000 contacts before attempting to activate Flows. This ensures your account has the quality \nhistory Meta needs.\nCTWA advantage: This is the biggest cost lever for WeNext clients running CTWA ads. Messages \nwithin the 72-hour FEP window are completely free of Meta\u2019s per-message charges."
