        "answer": "This document discusses the basics of large language models (LLMs), including tokenization, self-attention, and the transformer architecture. It also touches on challenges such as cost, latency, and context limits, and mentions future directions like multimodal understanding and agentic AI.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 2,
            "text": "\"Understanding\" might become two tokens: \"Understand\" and \"ing\". Everything the model does is built \non top of this next-token prediction loop, repeated thousands of times to generate a full response. \nWhat makes this powerful is scale: trained on trillions of tokens of text with billions of parameters (the \nmodel's internal, learned weights), the model implicitly absorbs grammar, facts, reasoning patterns, and \ncoding conventions \u2014 not because it was told the rules, but because predicting text well requires \nlearning them. \n3. The Transformer Architecture \n3.0 Origins: \u201cAttention Is All You Need\u201d \nThe transformer was introduced in a 2017 paper titled \u201cAttention Is All You Need,\u201d published by a team"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 5,
            "text": "6 \n3.2 Self-Attention \u2014 \"Who Is This Word Talking To?\" \nSelf-attention is the mechanism that lets every word in a sentence look at every other word and decide \nhow much to \"pay attention\" to it when building its own meaning. In the sentence \"The trophy didn't fit \nin the suitcase because it was too big,\" attention is what allows the model to correctly link \"it\" to \n\"trophy\" rather than \"suitcase\" \u2014 by weighing the relevance of every other word in context. \nPlain-English version \nAttention is a dynamic, learned weighting system \u2014 for every word, it asks \"which other words in this \nsentence matter most to understanding me?\" and blends their information in accordingly. \n3.3 Multi-Head Attention \nRather than computing attention once, the model runs several attention computations in parallel"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 4,
            "text": "The rest of this section walks through each stage of that diagram. \n3.1 Tokenization & Embeddings \nText is first broken into tokens, then each token is converted into a vector \u2014 a long list of numbers \u2014 \ncalled an embedding. This is not arbitrary: the model learns to place words with similar meaning close \ntogether in this numerical space. This same idea, applied to whole chunks of text rather than single \ntokens, is what powers semantic search \u2014 it turns a document into vectors so that passages with similar \nmeaning end up near each other, regardless of whether they share exact keywords."
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 14,
            "text": "careful system design to correct for at the application level. \n\u2022 Cost & latency: large models demand significant compute and energy; Section 5.6 covers the \nspecific levers used to manage this in production. \n\u2022 Context limits: very long documents still challenge even frontier models \u2014 a large context window \n(Section 5.4) does not guarantee the model weighs every part of it equally well. \n9.2 What's Next \nAlongside these challenges, four directions are shaping where LLMs, and the systems built around them, \nare headed next. \nDirection What it means \nMultimodal Text, image, audio, and video understanding unified in \na single model rather than separate systems. \nAgentic AI Autonomous task execution and planning, chaining \ntools and decisions with less human orchestration."
