            "text": "careful system design to correct for at the application level. \n\u2022 Cost & latency: large models demand significant compute and energy; Section 5.6 covers the \nspecific levers used to manage this in production. \n\u2022 Context limits: very long documents still challenge even frontier models \u2014 a large context window \n(Section 5.4) does not guarantee the model weighs every part of it equally well. \n9.2 What's Next \nAlongside these challenges, four directions are shaping where LLMs, and the systems built around them, \nare headed next. \nDirection What it means \nMultimodal Text, image, audio, and video understanding unified in \na single model rather than separate systems. \nAgentic AI Autonomous task execution and planning, chaining \ntools and decisions with less human orchestration."
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 2,
            "text": "3 \n1. Executive Summary \nA large language model (LLM) is a neural network trained on vast amounts of text to predict the next \nword in a sequence. That simple objective, at sufficient scale, produces a system that can write, reason, \nsummarize, translate, and hold conversations \u2014 and, when connected to a company's own data, act as \nthe reasoning engine behind production tools. \nThis document explains how LLMs work end to end: how they represent language, how they are trained, \nhow they are served in production, and how to decide between approaches like retrieval-augmented \ngeneration (RAG) and fine-tuning. Concepts are illustrated with concrete, realistic examples of how \nproduction LLM systems are typically built, so the ideas translate directly into practical design decisions."
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 9,
            "text": "needed to answer it. It degrades due to vocabulary mismatch between the query and source wording, \nchunk boundaries that cut a passage mid-thought, semantic drift in very large chunks, or stale duplicate \ndocuments competing with the current version in the index. It's controlled with hybrid search \n(combining vector similarity with keyword matching), a reranking step over a wider candidate set, and \nchunk sizes tuned per document type. \nGroundedness (faithfulness) measures whether the model's answer is actually supported by the \nretrieved context, rather than the model filling gaps from its own general knowledge, which may be \noutdated or invented. A response can be fluent and even factually correct in general and still be"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 9,
            "text": "outdated or invented. A response can be fluent and even factually correct in general and still be \nungrounded, meaning it wasn't really derived from what was retrieved. This is improved by prompting \nthe model to answer only from the provided context and say so when context is insufficient, attaching \ncitations to each claim, and running an automated check that compares the answer against the \nretrieved chunks. \nAnswer relevance is a separate axis again: an answer can be perfectly grounded in the retrieved text \nand still fail to address what the user actually asked, for example answering a related but different \nquestion. This is typically measured by comparing the semantic similarity of the generated answer back \nto the original query. \nWhy these metrics matter together"
          }
