        "sources": [
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 3,
            "text": "explicitly trained for \nReasoning, coding, and instruction-\nfollowing emerge from scale, not \nexplicit programming \n \nThis is the shift that turned language modeling from a collection of specialized NLP tools into a general-\npurpose reasoning and generation engine \u2014 and it's the reason a single model architecture can now sit \nunderneath a PDF question-answering system, a coding assistant, and a customer support agent, with"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 4,
            "text": "5 \nthe difference being data and orchestration around the model rather than a different model for each \njob. \nEvery modern LLM (GPT, Claude, Gemini, and LLaMA among them) is built on this same underlying \narchitecture. At a high level, text flows through it in a consistent pipeline: it is tokenized, converted into \nnumerical embeddings, passed through a stack of identical \u201ctransformer blocks\u201d that repeatedly refine \nthe model's understanding, and finally converted back into a probability distribution over the next \npossible token. The diagram below shows that full path. \n \nFigure 1 \u2014 End-to-end flow of a transformer: from raw text to the next predicted token. \nThe rest of this section walks through each stage of that diagram. \n3.1 Tokenization & Embeddings"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 8,
            "text": "model can reason about \u2014 the conversation, retrieved documents, instructions \u2014 must fit inside this \nwindow. A larger window doesn't guarantee better use of that space; models can still lose track of \ndetails buried in the middle of a very long context. \nTemperature: controls how much randomness is applied when choosing the next token. A low \ntemperature (around 0.1) makes output deterministic and repeatable, favoring the single most likely \ntoken every time \u2014 useful for factual or structured tasks. A high temperature (1.0 or above) allows less-\nlikely tokens to be chosen more often, producing more varied, creative, but less predictable output. \nTop-p (nucleus sampling): a complementary control that samples only from the smallest set of tokens"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 6,
            "text": "dollars for frontier models. \n4.2 Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) \nThe pretrained model is then shown curated examples of instruction-response pairs \u2014 a question and a \nwell-written answer \u2014 so it learns the format of being an assistant rather than just a text-completion \nengine. This is the stage that turns a raw language model into something that behaves like a chatbot. \n4.3 Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) \nHumans rank multiple model responses to the same prompt from best to worst. A reward model is \ntrained on those rankings, and the LLM is then further trained to produce outputs that the reward \nmodel scores highly. This is the stage most responsible for a model feeling helpful, honest, and safe \nrather than just fluent. \nWhy leadership should care about this pipeline"
          }
        ]
