            "text": "\"Understanding\" might become two tokens: \"Understand\" and \"ing\". Everything the model does is built \non top of this next-token prediction loop, repeated thousands of times to generate a full response. \nWhat makes this powerful is scale: trained on trillions of tokens of text with billions of parameters (the \nmodel's internal, learned weights), the model implicitly absorbs grammar, facts, reasoning patterns, and \ncoding conventions \u2014 not because it was told the rules, but because predicting text well requires \nlearning them. \n3. The Transformer Architecture \n3.0 Origins: \u201cAttention Is All You Need\u201d \nThe transformer was introduced in a 2017 paper titled \u201cAttention Is All You Need,\u201d published by a team"
          },
          {
            "source": "LLM.pdf",
            "page": 14,
            "text": "15 \n9. Challenges & The Road Ahead \nNo production LLM deployment is free of trade-offs. Being explicit about the current limits \u2014 and where \nthe field is headed \u2014 is part of using this technology responsibly. \n9.1 Current Challenges \n\u2022 Hallucinations: models generate plausible but incorrect information, because generation is \nfundamentally a statistical prediction of the next token, not a database lookup. This is inherent to \nhow LLMs work, not a bug that gets fully patched away. \n\u2022 Bias & safety: training data reflects the biases present in the text it was drawn from, which \nrequires deliberate alignment work (see Section 4.3, RLHF) to correct for at the model level, and \ncareful system design to correct for at the application level."
